CAT = 'iept2Safety'; SEG = 'SAF'
Q = [
('The HSA lists which of these jobs as hot work?',
 'Brazing a copper joint', ['Threading steel pipe by hand', 'Cutting PVC waste pipe', 'Tightening a compression nut'],
 'The HSA defines hot work as work that may generate enough heat, sparks or flame to cause a fire, and names welding, flame cutting, soldering, brazing and grinding. Hand threading uses cutting oil, not a flame.'),
('After a hot work job ends, when should the final fire check of the area be made?',
 'At least 60 minutes later', ['Straight away, before leaving', 'At least 15 minutes later', 'The next working morning'],
 'HSA hot work controls include a final check of the area at least 60 minutes after the job is completed, and certainly before the premises are left empty. Smouldering timber can take time to show.'),
('Under a hot work permit, who watches for fire and handles the extinguisher?',
 'A separate fire watcher', ['The plumber doing the brazing', 'The person who signed the permit', 'Nobody, if an extinguisher is close'],
 'The HSA list of hot work controls includes a separate person to fire-watch and use fire-fighting equipment. The person doing the brazing is looking at the joint, not the joists.'),
('When does the HSA say hot work should be allowed at all?',
 'When nothing safer will do', ['Whenever a permit is signed', 'Only outside working hours', 'Once a fire watcher arrives'],
 'The HSA advice is to identify all hot work and only allow it if there is no satisfactory alternative, such as a compression or press joint. A permit controls hot work; it does not justify it.'),
('Before soldering beside timber joists, what does a hot work system require?',
 'Remove or protect combustibles', ['Turn the torch to a smaller flame', 'Keep a bucket of water nearby only', 'Work quickly to limit the heat'],
 'Hot work controls include removing or protecting combustible and flammable materials and controlling sparks and heat. A smaller flame still ignites dry timber.'),
('Why must oil and grease be kept off oxygen regulators and fittings?',
 'They can ignite in pure oxygen', ['They make the valve threads seize', 'They wear the regulator seat', 'They clog the flashback arrestor'],
 'The HSA warns that pure oxygen at high pressure can react violently with oil and grease, and says never to use them on oxygen equipment.'),
('How should the valve of an oxygen cylinder be opened?',
 'Slowly', ['Quickly, all the way', 'Fully, then back a quarter turn', 'In one quick part turn'],
 'HSA oxygen safeguards: always open oxygen cylinder valves slowly. A fast opening heats the gas at the regulator seat.'),
('Air holds about 21% oxygen. A leaking hose raises a small room to 24%. What changes?',
 'Fires start more easily', ['Breathing becomes difficult', 'Nothing until it reaches 30%', 'Flames burn cooler and slower'],
 'The HSA notes that even a small rise to 24% oxygen makes fires easier to start and they burn hotter and more fiercely.'),
('A confined space feels stuffy. Why must oxygen never be released to freshen the air?',
 'It creates an enriched fire risk', ['It raises carbon monoxide levels', 'It displaces the nitrogen too fast', 'It sets off the gas detector'],
 'The HSA says oxygen should never be used to sweeten the air in a confined space: enriched air lets clothing and hair catch fire readily.'),
('In an oxygen-enriched atmosphere, which materials will burn vigorously?',
 'Nearly all, even metals', ['Oils and greases, but not metals', 'Paper, timber and cloth, not rubber', 'Plastics and rubber, not textiles'],
 'The HSA notes that nearly all materials, including textiles, rubber and even metals, will burn vigorously in oxygen.'),
('An LPG cylinder for a soldering torch holds which gas?',
 'Propane or butane', ['Acetylene', 'Natural gas (methane)', 'Oxygen and propane'],
 'LPG is propane or butane, both named by the HSA as flammable gases. Acetylene is the fuel gas used with oxygen for welding and cutting.'),
('Where should spare gas cylinders be kept on a job?',
 'In a marked separate store', ['Beside the work area, upright', 'In the van, with windows shut', 'In the basement, out of the way'],
 'HSA fire prevention advice is to provide clearly marked separate storage for gas cylinders, outside or separated from the workplace, not stockpiled where the work is.'),
("You must cut a bracket off an 'empty' drum that held oil. What should happen first?",
 'Gas-free and clean the drum', ['Purge it with oxygen', 'Shake it to check it is empty', 'Leave the bung out for an hour'],
 "An HSA safety alert followed a fatal explosion during hot work on an 'empty' 200 litre drum. Residue vapour needs gas-freeing, cleaning or inerting first, or a specialist."),
('Under the Safety, Health and Welfare at Work Act 2005, an apprentice who finds a damaged ladder should:',
 'Report it straight away', ['Report it at the next toolbox talk', 'Repair it, then carry on', 'Report it at the end of the week'],
 'Section 13 of the 2005 Act requires an employee to report any defect in the place of work, system of work or equipment that might endanger anyone, as soon as practicable.'),
('Under the 2005 Act, what must an employee do with protective equipment the employer provides?',
 'Use it as trained', ['Wear it when the job looks risky', 'Adapt it to fit if needed', 'Use it when the foreman is on site'],
 'Section 13(1)(g) requires employees to make correct use of protective clothing and equipment, having regard to their training and instructions.'),
('Which duty does the 2005 Act place on employees about intoxicants?',
 'Not to work impaired by them', ['To tell the HSA about prescribed drugs', 'To take a test before each shift', 'To avoid them only on scaffolds'],
 'Section 13(1)(b) says an employee must not be under the influence of an intoxicant to the extent that they endanger themselves or others.'),
('Your employer books a safety course for you. Under the 2005 Act, you must:',
 'Attend as reasonably required', ['Attend only if it is in work hours', 'Attend only if it gives a certificate', 'Attend only once Phase 2 is passed'],
 'Section 13(1)(f) requires employees to attend training and undergo assessment the employer reasonably requires on safety, health and welfare.'),
('Horseplay with a blowtorch on site breaches which employee duty?',
 'Avoiding improper conduct', ['Reporting defects promptly', 'Attending safety training', 'Declaring past injuries'],
 'Section 13(1)(e) bars improper conduct or other behaviour likely to endanger the employee or anyone else at work.'),
('Hot work controls say a job should be left how when the work stops?',
 'Clean and safe', ['With tools left ready to restart', 'With the permit kept open', 'Swept of metal offcuts only'],
 'The HSA hot work list ends with leaving the workplace clean and safe, then a final check of the area at least an hour later.'),
('Before digging a trench for a water service, which HSA code should be followed?',
 'Avoiding Danger From Underground Services', ['Code of Practice for Working in Confined Spaces', 'Code of Practice for Fire Safety of Furnishings', 'Guide to Part 8: Explosive Atmospheres'],
 'The HSA points to its Code of Practice for Avoiding Danger From Underground Services, and to Dial Before You Dig, before excavation near buried gas and electric cables.'),
]
